[MATH: \eqalign{
\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \dfrac{\color{red}-9}{8} \[5pt]
3 \cdot 8 &= 2x \cdot (-9) \[5pt]
24 &= -18x
}]

First, note that the denominator [MATH: 2x+1] cannot be zero, since division by [MATH: 0] is undefined. Therefore we cannot have [MATH: 2x+1=0,] which means [MATH: x=-\dfrac{1}{2}] cannot be a solution.

To solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:

[MATH: \eqalign{
\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} &= \dfrac 9 8 \[5pt]
3x \cdot 8 &= (2x+1) \cdot 9 \[5pt]
24x &= 18x + 9
}]

Then, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:

[MATH: \eqalign{
24x &= 18x + 9 \[5pt]
24x-18x &= 18x + 9 -18x \[5pt]
6x& = 9 \[5pt]
\dfrac{6x}{6} & = \dfrac{9}{6} \[5pt]
x &= \dfrac{3}{2}
}]

Since [MATH: x = \dfrac{3}{2}] is different from [MATH: x=-\dfrac{1}{2}], we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is [MATH: x=\dfrac{3}{2}.]
